{
 "task": "p2: two players, no Mercy rule (-mercy 1000), default rules otherwise (RULES.md sections 1-4, 7)",
 "found": false,
 "players": 2,
 "end_rule": "both",
 "end_rule_note": "With two players the game ends at the first exit under both endings (official: first empty hand wins; section 7: play until one player is left, which with two players is the same moment). One answer covers both.",
 "verdict": "IMPOSSIBLE. No legal 2-player history reaches a forced endless loop. A forced loop here means a cycle of states in which no player ever has a decision with two or more options. The candidate loop (Red/Blue Reverse pair) is a genuine forced 2-cycle once it is on the table, but no legal history can reach it. The same holds for every other forced loop, including the Skip, Skip Everyone, 7-swap and 0-pass variants.",
 "consequence": "For two players, the route used for 4-6 players (one explicit history into a forced loop, so P(never ends) > 0 and E[length] = infinity under random play) is closed. This result does not by itself prove P(never ends) = 0 for 2 players: a closed never-ending class that needs random choices or random reshuffles is not excluded by this argument. None has been seen either (0 proven cycles, 0 stuck, 0 capped games in all 2-player runs listed below).",
 "rules_used": "Defaults of sim/nomercy.c (= RULES.md): must play if able (voluntary_draw 0), play the card that ends draw-until-playable (after_draw 0), Reverse = Skip with 2 players (reverse2p_skip 1), stacking any Draw Card of value >= the last one, colour irrelevant (stack_rule 0), 7 = swap and 0 = pass hands (zero_seven 1), Wild Reverse Draw 4 hits its own player with 2 players, a wild's colour and the Roulette colour are named by the player. Deck 0 (168 cards: 4 x Wild Draw 10, 8 x Roulette, 8 x Wild Reverse Draw 4, 4 x Wild Draw 6).",
 "definitions": {
  "decision": "Any point where the random policy has 2 or more options. These are: 2 or more distinct playable types on a free turn; any wild play (colour: 4 options); a Roulette victim naming the colour (4 options); facing a penalty while holding a stackable card (stack or accept); a 7-swap target with 3 or more players.",
  "forced cycle": "A periodic sequence of states (hands, piles, top, colour, turn, direction, penalty) with no decision anywhere in it. Reshuffles of 2 or more cards are also excluded, as in the simulator's -detect_cycles, but the proof never needs this.",
  "hand": "A multiset of cards, tracked as it moves between seats: a 7 swaps two hands and a 0 passes them on without mixing them."
 },
 "proof": [
  "Step 1 (no wild is ever played in a forced cycle). Playing any wild needs a colour choice: the player names it, or, for a Roulette, the victim does. Both are 4-option decisions.",
  "Step 2 (draws happen, so every loose card cycles through the hands). A cycle with plays but no draws would shrink a hand, so it could not repeat. A cycle with neither plays nor draws is two players both unable to play with nothing to draw. That is impossible: the 24 wilds cannot all be the single top card, so some hand holds a wild, and on its free turn the wild is playable. So there are draws in each period. The draw pile must then be refilled by a reshuffle in each period, so every card below the top gets drawn within a period. Each drawn card must leave that hand again for the state to repeat, so it is played later.",
  "Step 3 (all 24 wilds stay in hands). A loose wild would be drawn (step 2) and then played, which contradicts step 1. The top card changes every period, so a wild cannot stay on top either.",
  "Step 4 (one hand W holds every wild and never acts; the other hand H acts on every turn, always a free turn, and holds no wild). A hand holding a Wild Draw 10 can never act in the cycle. On a free turn the wild is playable (lemma L2), so the turn is a choice or a colour choice. Facing a penalty, the Wild Draw 10 can be stacked (L2), so stack-or-accept is a choice. If the 4 Wild Draw 10s were split between both hands, nobody could act. So one hand, W, holds all four and never acts, and H takes every turn. H is never under a penalty, because only H plays, and a Draw Card played by H hands the next turn to W. So H always has free turns. A wild held by H would then be an option (a choice, or a colour choice if it is the only option), so H holds no wild and W holds all 24.",
  "Step 5 (the cycle's cards). Only H plays, and the next turn must again be H's on a free turn with W untouched. By lemma L3, the cards H plays in the cycle ('cycling types') are therefore all of kind Skip, Skip Everyone, Reverse, 0 or 7. The cards H holds but never plays ('junk types') are unplayable on every top that occurs in the cycle; otherwise H would face a choice. By lemma L1 (playability between coloured cards is symmetric), a junk type and a cycling type never share a colour or a kind.",
  "Step 6 (W has no last change: contradiction). W starts as a 7-card hand and must end up with 24 or more cards, so it changes at least once. Let t* be its LAST change (a play or a draw). After t*, only H plays, W never draws, and every card outside W stays inside H, the draw pile, the discard pile or the top. So every card on top after t* ends up either as a junk type in H or as a cycling type. A wild on top would later be drawn by H, which steps 3-4 rule out. Consecutive tops T_i, T_{i+1} share a colour or a kind, because T_{i+1} is played on T_i on a free turn. The one exception is a stacking reply right after t*, when W's last change was playing a Draw Card: that reply is itself a Draw Card, not a cycling type, and W can then only accept with an empty pile, so the chain from it is all junk. A junk type is never adjacent to a cycling type (step 5), yet the tops are cycling types from the cycle's start onwards. So the top T0 right after t* is a cycling type, i.e. of kind Skip, Skip Everyone, Reverse, 0 or 7. Lemma L4 checks every possible last change of W. Accepting a penalty leaves a Draw Card on top. A Roulette reveal leaves a wild on top. A stacking play or a Discard All leaves a card whose kind is not in the list. Drawing until playable ends with a play. W playing a Skip, Skip Everyone or Reverse makes W move again. W playing a 7 or 0 passes W to the seat that moves next. In the last two cases W is on a free turn holding a playable wild, so it must play and change again, which contradicts 'last'. Hence no forced cycle is reachable. QED."
 ],
 "why_the_candidate_fails": "The candidate: A to move, top Red Reverse, only Blue Reverse loose, A holding only Yellow/Green non-Reverse cards, B holding everything else. Its only possible predecessor is a play of a Reverse onto the other Reverse. The top before that card becomes a loose card that A later draws, so it must be the other Reverse (a junk card would share nothing with a Reverse). Going back move by move, the first Reverse of the chain must have been played by B, the hand with the wilds, as its last change. With 2 players a Reverse acts as a Skip, so B moves again. B holds wilds, so it must play again. So B's hand never stops changing before the loop, and the loop is never entered. A 'junk flood' that absorbs extra loose cards into A's hand does not help: each extra loose card must be junk, and junk cannot sit next to a Reverse in the chain of tops.",
